  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CSA-GCA</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">GCA, co-applicants &#x2013; actively contributed to co-design the proposed action and will act as central actors in the implementation phase of the project. Not only the two co-applicants shall build on expertise and longstanding experience in implementing similar project including activities aiming to build capacities of CSOs to effectively implement advocacy actions, but they shall also strongly benefit from their establishment in target areas and trusted relation with the grassroot level; including rural farmer communities. GCA will be responsible for implementing activities specific to SO1 and SO2 with respective CSOs Group 1 and Group 2. Together with Oxfam in Laos, co-applicants shall contribute to designing tailored training modules and conduct ToTs (OUTPUT 1, OUTPUT 2), establishing a strategic plan and implement the alliance advocacy campaign (OUTPUT 2, OUTPUT 3), as well as supervising the sound implementation of the third-party mechanism by alliance member CSOs (OUTPUT 3, OUTPUT 4, OUTPUT 6). GCA will support alliance building and influencing campaigning, while the more technical aspect of their mandate shall be supported by a research institute and experts fully committed to implementing CSA-specific activities along with target communities and beneficiaries.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Green Community Alliance (GCA )</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Green community alliance (GCA) is a local non-profit association. The main vision of the association is to deliver land/natural resources-based policy and legal awareness for rural communities of Laos. GCA has a role in strengthening rural ethnic communities# knowledge with regards to conservation and sustainable use of natural resources including land; promoting sustainable livelihoods and reforestation.
GCA is a relatively small and young organization if compared with others Civil Society Organizations at both national and regional level.  The organization currently has 8 staff members and over 10 of volunteers who have graduated from bachelor and master degrees in relevant subject areas including: forestry, environmental management, human and social sciences.
In line with our organization capacity building policies, GCA supports their team leader and other staff members to participate in the various training courses at national and international such as: CCA and Improving Forest Governance in CIDT-England</narrative>
  </participating-org>
